            "A hot-air balloon is one of the simplest flying machines ever built, and in a sense it does not fly at all. It floats, in the way a cork floats in water, because the air trapped inside its envelope has been heated and is therefore lighter, volume for volume, than the cooler air around it. As long as the difference is large enough to carry the weight of the fabric, the basket and the people in it, the balloon rises.",
            "The first balloons of this kind were built in France by Joseph-Michel and Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier, two brothers from a family of paper makers in the town of Annonay. They experimented with bags of paper and cloth held over fires, and in 1783 they were invited to show their invention to the king at Versailles. Nobody yet knew whether a living creature could survive in the sky, so the first passengers were a sheep, a duck and a rooster. The three animals rose above the crowd, drifted for a few minutes, and came down safely in a nearby wood.",
            "Two months later, in November of the same year, two men made the first free flight carrying people. Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d’Arlandes rose from a garden on the edge of Paris and crossed the city, feeding straw to a fire that hung beneath the open neck of the balloon. Only days afterwards a rival design rose from Paris as well, lifted not by hot air but by hydrogen gas.",
            "Modern hot-air balloons look much the same from a distance, but the details have changed. The envelope is sewn from nylon or polyester, the basket is still usually woven from wicker because it is light and absorbs the shock of landing, and the fire has been replaced by propane burners that roar for a few seconds at a time.",
