            "This year, for the first time, she had help. The supper had grown too large for her kitchen staff alone, so a dozen guests had volunteered to carry the dishes, and they arrived early in borrowed aprons, eager and slightly nervous. Marguerite showed them how to hold three bowls at once, which tables to start with, and where to stack the empty plates. Then she went back to her stove, where a pot of leek and potato soup the size of a barrel was coming slowly to the boil.",
            "Everything went well until the soup. The volunteers had just begun to carry it out when the chairman of the parish council, a cheerful man named Arthur Pyle, stood up at the top table and tapped his glass. Nobody had warned him about Marguerite’s rule, or perhaps he had forgotten it. He thanked the farmers, and then the volunteers, and then the farmers again, and then he began a long story about a prize-winning marrow that had been stolen from the show in 1987. The volunteers, not liking to walk about during a speech, put down their trays by the wall and waited for him to finish.",
            "By the time Arthur sat down, a quarter of an hour had passed. The guests served the soup first complained that it was cold, and when Marguerite came out of the kitchen to taste it, she found that they were right.",
